WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.030
Making a direct sequel to a moving decades
later is becoming more common recently.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:17.180
It presents a unique challenge for directors
and cinematographer as they have to find the

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.310
balance between creating something that feels
connected to the original film while still

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:29.529
creating something that feel unique and new.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:35.570
Here is Denis Villeneuve, the director of
Blade Runner 2049 describing his relationship

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.320
to the original Blade Runner film…

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:18.360
Denis and Director of Photography Roger Deakins develop a unique
visual style, updating the world and the production

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:26.310
design for a story that takes place long after
the original events.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:32.630
Roger Deakins often abandons the hard high
contrast lighting of the original for more

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.700
soft and diffused lighting.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:42.939
Production design has been updated to reflect
the changes in the world.

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:47.579
But there are still visual connections, the
ones that I’ve highlighted here that ground

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:50.959
2049 in a familiar space.

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:56.810
Re-occurring places feel similar, and visual
motifs are used to link past and present characters

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.740
and themes.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:08.209
Threading the needle of being original while
remaining true to the source material is difficult.

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:14.439
I think where 2049 succeeds is by making these
references subtly enough that it doesn’t

00:02:14.439 --> 00:02:16.980
feel formulaic or forced.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.890
Most of the visual references are made during
segments of the film that are establishing

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.580
or transitioning shots.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:30.970
2049 doesn’t lean on those references to
be the weight of the film, instead it uses

00:02:30.970 --> 00:03:21.099
them to allude to

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:34.730
a familiar place and world, while building
towards

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:53.700
a story that is fresh.

